## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over multiple segments with a budget and in/out state tracking to allow adjacency.

## Key Concepts
- DP with segment count and in/out state
- Transitions for starting, extending, and ending a segment
- Handling adjacency and at-most-k constraint

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(a, k){
  const n = a.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  // dp[i][t][s] -> max sum using first i elements
  let dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> [NEG, NEG]));
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      for(let s=0;s<2;s++){
        const cur = dp[i][t][s];
        if(cur===NEG) continue;
        if(s===0){
          // skip a[i]
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
          // start new
          if(t<k) dp[i+1][t+1][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t+1][1], cur + a[i]);
        } else {
          // extend
          dp[i+1][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][1], cur + a[i]);
          // end
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = 0;
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++) ans = Math.max(ans, dp[n][t][0], dp[n][t][1]);
  return ans;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend Kadane-like DP to k non-overlapping segments with per-element penalties and reconstruction of exact endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP for k disjoint subarrays with penalties
- Local/global state separation for endings vs global maxima
- Backpointer reconstruction to recover endpoints
- Time complexity O(nk), space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, p) {
  const n = A.length;
  const negInf = -1e18;
  const local = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  const global = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  global[0][0] = 0;
  // backpointers omitted for brevity in this snippet
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const val = A[i - 1] - p;
      local[i][t] = Math.max(local[i - 1][t] + val, global[i - 1][t - 1] + val);
      global[i][t] = Math.max(global[i - 1][t], local[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return { maxValue: global[n][k], endpoints: [] };
}
```

